WEBVTT

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.510
 Delicious Revolution is brought to you by us, by me and Chelsea, specifically by our consulting business.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:11.330
 We provide participatory research, communications, and creative work to organizations that are building food movements.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:14.190
 What's your food movement? Let us know and get in touch.

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:34.330
 This is where I think Meal Ticket was super effective, like on a deeper political level, because I sat people across from cross-departmentally and cross-hierarchically.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:40.740
 So, you know, there might be a security guard across from a curator, an educator across from a janitor, etc., etc.

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:44.490
 And I brought everybody up to the boardroom and made them a really nice lunch.

00:00:44.830 --> 00:00:48.690
 I even had to get new rules made for the security guard so that they could take an hour lunch.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:01:01.730
 And then through that recipe exchange, they got to know each other and got to, you know, have a sort of cooperative situation where, at the time, it was complained to me that there were really serious silos in the different departments at the museum.

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:07.850
 So, this was a chance to kind of just break that down, even for like a temporary utopic hour a month.

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:16.390
 Welcome to Delicious Revolution, a show about food, culture, and place.

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:17.450
 I'm Chelsea Wills.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:19.050
 And I'm Devin Sampson.

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:29.610
 We talk with people who work in and think about all aspects of food, including farmers, chefs, artists, and activists, bringing you in-depth conversations with some amazing people.

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:45.770
 On this third season of Delicious Revolution, we're bringing you stories and perspectives from unseen places in food systems, going behind kitchen doors, into underground nests of native bees, under the waves, and to the faraway origins of flavors we love, just to name a few.

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:51.900
 I'm speaking with people who work with food in places we normally cannot see or don't notice.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:54.520
 It's a season of unseen stories of food.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:58.640
 You can subscribe to Delicious Revolution on iTunes or your favorite podcast app.

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:02.860
 Just search for Delicious Revolution wherever you get your podcasts, and you'll find us.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:10.360
 Our website is deliciousrevolutionshow.com, where we have pictures, links, and more information about all the people on our show.

00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:14.160
 Lexa Walsh is a longtime artist and cultural worker based in the Bay Area.

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:17.540
 She has also lived, worked, and exhibited, and toured internationally.

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:22.970
 She founded the experimental music, performance, and film venue, the Heinz Afterworld Lounge.

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:28.110
 She worked for many years as a curator and administrator at SESTA International Art Center in the Czech Republic.

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:35.350
 And she founded and organizes Oakland Stock, a branch of the Sunday Soup Network micro-granting dinner series.

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:40.810
 Her silo-busting project, Meal Ticket, brings people who don't normally eat with each other together for a meal.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:43.500
 Here's Chelsea's interview with Lexa Walsh.

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:46.600
 You have a lot of cookbooks here.

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:55.240
 Maybe we should just start talking about this big pile of cookbooks we have in front of us right now.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:56.540
 Welcome to the show, Lexa.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:56.880
 Thanks.

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:58.340
 Chelsea, nice to be here.

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:06.040
 So my graduate thesis at PSU was a cookbook called All Day Singing and Dinner on the Ground.

00:03:06.860 --> 00:03:14.450
 I actually, it took me a long time to figure out what my thesis project would be, of course, because I did so much work and had lots of interest.

00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:19.790
 But one consistent thing was that I did and started food projects when I was in graduate school.

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:26.010
 I'd been a chef and been an artist and everybody was trying to convince me that I needed to combine the two.

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:27.810
 And I thought, no, no, I can't do it.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:34.510
 And I finally did it, the first one, because I was the caterer for our Monday night lecture series.

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:39.510
 So I fed all of the students and the visiting artists and the professors every Monday night.

00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:47.430
 And I had, I started this idea, which is now called Meal Ticket, but then it was just in its preliminary stage.

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:57.180
 And I sat people across from each other and asked them to do a recipe exchange with each other, where the person speaking had to tell the recipe to the person across from them.

00:03:57.220 --> 00:03:59.440
 And that person had to write down the recipe.

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:07.740
 So there was an active listening and active communicating involved rather than a passing of information that was not actually shared.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:12.860
 And then that got, I printed it into a little zine and gave it out the next week.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:15.080
 And people were into it.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:21.020
 And they were really funny recipes because another thing that I always do is ask people for recipes for anything.

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:23.680
 They don't have to be, they don't have to feel like they're cooks.

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:28.860
 It can be a recipe for disaster, a recipe for a marriage or recipe for anything.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:36.410
 Just a set of instructions that then reveals somebody's values and culture and identity and personality.

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:40.000
 So I've done this now in like a bunch of different settings.

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:47.650
 And some have been a lot more, they're always successful in terms of community building and conversation building.

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:53.500
 But sometimes they're actually really effective in like in a more deep political way.

00:04:55.900 --> 00:05:01.940
 So this started more like it started as this kind of tiny conceptual project, right?

00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:05.750
 To ask for participation with people?

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:15.530
 It was, yeah, basically a way to get our classmates talking to each other through recipes.

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:18.160
 Yeah, just a really kind of simple.

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:24.040
 Also because our Monday night framework was that somebody had to do a presentation.

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:31.260
 And so what usually was happening is that people were eating their food while watching somebody talk about something.

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:38.120
 And I hate it when people eat and don't talk to each other instead or, you know, it's like watching TV and eating.

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:40.200
 Just I'm not really down with that.

00:05:40.600 --> 00:05:42.180
 My family does that.

00:05:42.260 --> 00:05:42.960
 It drives me crazy.

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:51.840
 So, yeah, it was a way to also just sort of like to shake things up a little bit in our program and how the Monday night.

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:52.580
 Oh, and then.

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:55.720
 But then it turned into like lots of other food projects.

00:05:55.860 --> 00:05:56.030
 Yes.

00:05:56.270 --> 00:05:56.570
 Okay.

00:05:56.750 --> 00:05:59.310
 And so that's what this big sack of cookbooks is.

00:05:59.530 --> 00:06:02.290
 You're like, oh, they actually do go together sometimes.

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:04.160
 Yeah.

00:06:04.220 --> 00:06:06.320
 So then that was something.

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:18.380
 So then I ended up doing a cookbook that was with a fellow student, Robin Corbo, where I just made a series of recipes based on what I served those Monday nights.

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:20.600
 And she did illustrations of the visiting artists.

00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:22.400
 So that was called the Monday night cookbook.

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:31.500
 And then I, cooking conversations was the first name of pre, of meal ticket, pre meal ticket.

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:44.440
 And then I did, I got this great residency at the Portland Art Museum in the education department, which has totally done great things for me, that residency in many ways.

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:51.090
 But I did the meal ticket project with the staff there and did a monthly silo busting luncheon.

00:06:51.950 --> 00:07:02.130
 And that, this is where I think meal ticket was super effective, like on a deeper political level, because I sat people across from, cross departmentally and cross hierarchically.

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:09.370
 So, you know, there might be a security guard across from a curator, an educator across from a janitor, et cetera, et cetera.

00:07:09.530 --> 00:07:13.070
 And I brought everybody up to the boardroom and made them a really nice lunch.

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:18.440
 I even had to get new rules made for the security guard so that they could take an hour lunch.

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:36.240
 And then through that, that recipe exchange, they got to know each other and got to, you know, have a sort of cooperative situation where at the time it was complained to me that there were really serious silos in the different departments at the museum.

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:42.360
 So this was a chance to kind of just break that down, even for like a temporary utopic hour a month.

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:50.760
 And then it also led to the HR person starting silo busting activities after that.

00:07:51.380 --> 00:08:01.450
 So, you know, it's like political activism in the museum and some awesome, awesome recipes that I use all the time and some funny ones.

00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:05.520
 Like there are a lot of good, heavy drinkers in the Portland Art Museum staff.

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:09.770
 And like, here's a hangover remedy.

00:08:10.810 --> 00:08:13.710
 And there's also, you realize there are a lot of artists in the staff.

00:08:13.830 --> 00:08:16.210
 And it also plays on the docent cookbook idea.

00:08:16.290 --> 00:08:20.750
 So this was sold at the museum store for a few months.

00:08:21.330 --> 00:08:28.780
 And it's also nice to say like, hey, the staff here are people, valuable people who have ideas.

00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:31.890
 Then you should read them the rest of the world who comes into the museum.

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:37.030
 When you start those projects, what's the first meal like?

00:08:37.110 --> 00:08:38.690
 It's usually a little bit awkward.

00:08:39.330 --> 00:08:44.230
 I did it also with a bunch of seniors in New Smarter Beach, Florida.

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:49.060
 And it's funny with them because I always do it for free.

00:08:49.780 --> 00:08:56.180
 I mean, that's really important, I think, to offer it as a generous gift and not as a business idea.

00:08:56.180 --> 00:09:02.000
 So with seniors and a lot of people, free food is a no brainer, you know, it gets people together.

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:06.520
 But it was a little bit awkward because I didn't know about existing.

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:10.500
 This was in a small town and I did these meals every week for four months.

00:09:10.940 --> 00:09:17.470
 I didn't realize that there were some existing, like, very serious social issues going on.

00:09:17.810 --> 00:09:24.630
 For instance, certain people hating each other who had deeply offended each other for years and I sat them across from each other having no idea.

00:09:25.090 --> 00:09:31.630
 Or the fact that it was completely racially divided and that it was only white people who were showing up to this meal.

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:39.510
 And so I actually had to bring the meal to the black side of town to get half of the residents of the town to come to the meal.

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:47.860
 And it was really, you know, here I was, like, trying to address institutional racism with this project, which was naive of me.

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:54.090
 But also interesting to sort of at least bring that to light and talk about it.

00:09:55.780 --> 00:10:04.540
 And so I want to hear about all of these, but I'm curious, like, with this one at the Portland Art Museum, people see each other every day, right?

00:10:04.540 --> 00:10:14.350
 So I don't know if that was the same thing in Florida, but, like, I think about anything that happens more than once where people start to have more ownership over it.

00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:18.310
 Like, does it start off kind of awkward and then does it change over the course of a project?

00:10:18.750 --> 00:10:21.290
 How long did you do it there for and what was that like?

00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:24.680
 Yeah, so it becomes sort of a thing.

00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:31.850
 So at Portland Art Museum, there were a few people that were, like, everybody was on the mailing list, but there were a few people that would sign up first thing.

00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:34.570
 As soon as it came out, like, they were going to be there.

00:10:34.710 --> 00:10:44.640
 And so they got to also experience different people and, you know, some people that maybe they had also had issues with before, but they knew that this was a kind of safe place to be.

00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:55.860
 So it became, like, throughout the year, it became a kind of, like, popular, more and more popular and then coveted thing to do to some extent.

00:10:56.990 --> 00:11:02.920
 Whereas in Florida, it also became, like, people were trying to reserve for them and their friends.

00:11:02.980 --> 00:11:04.560
 And I was like, no, that's not how it works.

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:07.900
 You have to actually, like, come and talk to other people.

00:11:07.900 --> 00:11:09.930
 And that was interesting, too.

00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:22.430
 There were certain, like, insider groups of people that came to every meal and then there were other ones and trying to sort of cross them, also across class and interest.

00:11:22.750 --> 00:11:26.410
 But, yeah, like, by the end, it was very, I wouldn't say comfortable.

00:11:26.900 --> 00:11:28.680
 Maybe at Portland Art Museum, it became comfortable.

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:35.040
 In New Smyrna Beach in Florida, it probably became less comfortable because then I started dealing with the racism aspect.

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:44.110
 Also, this phrase, like, there's no such thing as a free lunch kind of comes up for me when I hear about this project because you actually are asking for an exchange, right?

00:11:44.150 --> 00:11:54.510
 And in some ways, like, it could be, like, really easy or it could be, like, really intense for people to, I mean, I guess, to, like, share a meal together or whatever.

00:11:54.610 --> 00:11:56.430
 I have to spend some time with some.

00:11:56.690 --> 00:12:04.170
 I can think of people like that that I've worked with before that I would, like, spending an hour with them eating would be, like, a long time.

00:12:05.740 --> 00:12:10.800
 What's kind of the range of those experiences that people had or that you observed, maybe?

00:12:11.440 --> 00:12:15.720
 Well, usually the first 10 minutes of every meal were kind of awkward.

00:12:16.370 --> 00:12:22.880
 And then when people just started, like, you know, I'd go in and grab the food and start bringing it out and serving it.

00:12:24.020 --> 00:12:28.980
 The next thing you know, people are blabbing and I can barely get a word in to even get them to do the recipe exchange.

00:12:29.060 --> 00:12:31.220
 Then you know that it's actually working.

00:12:32.500 --> 00:12:35.410
 And I think food and sitting at a table just does that.

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:42.370
 Maybe because I grew up in a family that we didn't all, I'm the youngest of 15 kids.

00:12:43.150 --> 00:12:50.090
 And we grew up in a big house, but the table, kitchen table is not big enough for all of us.

00:12:50.170 --> 00:12:51.430
 And everybody had different schedules.

00:12:51.430 --> 00:12:55.420
 So the first, like, youngest six kids would eat together.

00:12:55.680 --> 00:12:58.740
 Then the boys who came home from their basketball practice.

00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:02.640
 And then the girls who did their professional swimming came home at, like, 9 o'clock.

00:13:02.840 --> 00:13:07.060
 Then my parents would lock us out of the kitchen and they would have meat.

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:11.760
 Like, we'd have pancakes or fish sticks or something like that.

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:15.140
 They'd have, you know, their time together to kind of catch up.

00:13:15.550 --> 00:13:22.030
 So anyway, but we had a very, like, casual, jovial eating experience at home, even if the food wasn't particularly good.

00:13:22.350 --> 00:13:22.990
 No offense, Mom.

00:13:24.110 --> 00:13:29.720
 And I think for other people, their dinner, family dinner experience was really different.

00:13:29.860 --> 00:13:33.920
 So I think of, oh, yeah, every table is just warm and inviting.

00:13:34.080 --> 00:13:36.080
 And I know that that's not really true for some people.

00:13:36.180 --> 00:13:41.660
 I think some people's sitting at the table was silent and awkward and negative.

00:13:42.180 --> 00:13:47.260
 And so I want to acknowledge that that was, that is the experience for some people.

00:13:47.260 --> 00:13:51.540
 And there's an interesting artist, Takeshi Moro, who lives in Santa Clara.

00:13:51.540 --> 00:14:02.010
 He did a silent meal at Open Engagement a few years ago when I programmed the food, curated the food programming for lunchtime at Open Engagement.

00:14:02.010 --> 00:14:05.530
 I mean, it was 2013, maybe.

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:11.630
 And he did this really beautiful meal where everybody just sat in silence for an hour.

00:14:12.350 --> 00:14:15.030
 And I thought it was a really rich project.

00:14:15.170 --> 00:14:22.360
 I'm more, I have that same assumption as you, like, that a table is a warm place or it's like this place where things get worked out, I guess.

00:14:22.580 --> 00:14:26.260
 So I know I just said there are people I can think of who I wouldn't want to sit down with for an hour.

00:14:26.380 --> 00:14:27.520
 And that's definitely true.

00:14:27.790 --> 00:14:31.430
 But I'd rather sit down and eat with them than do almost anything else, right?

00:14:31.510 --> 00:14:36.790
 Of all the, I don't know, I want to have a meeting or, like, you know, even going for a walk.

00:14:37.050 --> 00:14:39.540
 But anyways, it creates this common ground.

00:14:39.620 --> 00:14:41.390
 I think that it feels really good to me.

00:14:41.890 --> 00:14:43.490
 That's kind of an interesting thing to play with.

00:14:44.130 --> 00:14:50.260
 And I mean, I don't know so much about your work, but I feel like that's something that kind of runs through these things, right?

00:14:50.320 --> 00:14:57.500
 Is this space and this container that you kind of hold at a table is a place where, like, things happen, that life gets lived.

00:14:58.420 --> 00:14:59.130
 Do you think that's true?

00:14:59.570 --> 00:14:59.910
 Absolutely.

00:15:00.250 --> 00:15:05.910
 Yeah, I mean, and it's also, I mean, we've been sitting together eating since the dawn of time.

00:15:05.910 --> 00:15:23.800
 So it's one thing that, you know, even if we are sitting down and eating a scrap, you know, I say this from a privileged place, but that sharing, it's just sort of like a basic human need to not only to eat, but to eat together.

00:15:25.310 --> 00:15:25.430
 Right.

00:15:25.590 --> 00:15:27.290
 And it has a lot of commonality, right?

00:15:27.370 --> 00:15:34.760
 I was, a few weeks ago, I was thinking about inviting some people over for dinner who have a really big house.

00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:38.280
 And I mean, my house probably would fit in their house like six times.

00:15:39.100 --> 00:15:46.620
 And my partner was saying to me, like, yeah, but think of all the times you have had dinner in someone's house that's been the size of your kitchen.

00:15:47.330 --> 00:15:49.250
 And there was still something to talk about.

00:15:49.450 --> 00:15:50.530
 And there was still food.

00:15:51.190 --> 00:15:53.450
 And sometimes you liked it and sometimes you didn't.

00:15:53.610 --> 00:15:55.350
 But, like, you knew how to do it.

00:15:55.390 --> 00:15:56.430
 And it's really nice.

00:15:56.430 --> 00:15:58.390
 It's like everybody gets to be an expert, right?

00:15:58.490 --> 00:16:02.890
 So even if it's, you're right, even if it's a scrap or even if it's McDonald's, it's still like it's food.

00:16:02.990 --> 00:16:04.010
 Like, it works.

00:16:04.900 --> 00:16:05.040
 Yeah.

00:16:05.120 --> 00:16:07.140
 And we all need it and we all want it.

00:16:07.220 --> 00:16:08.820
 And most of us love it.

00:16:08.900 --> 00:16:10.960
 And we got to do it a lot of times a day.

00:16:11.020 --> 00:16:11.720
 So it's easy.

00:16:12.040 --> 00:16:12.200
 Okay.

00:16:12.280 --> 00:16:18.410
 So meal ticket happened and you did it in Portland and then you did another residency in Florida.

00:16:18.750 --> 00:16:18.970
 Yeah.

00:16:19.130 --> 00:16:19.830
 Did it there.

00:16:19.930 --> 00:16:21.690
 It's like the major part of my residency.

00:16:22.050 --> 00:16:23.370
 And then I did it.

00:16:23.790 --> 00:16:24.930
 I've done it in different situations.

00:16:24.930 --> 00:16:33.800
 So I am the culinary artist in residence at Atlantic Center for the Arts now where I did that residency in 2013 for four months.

00:16:33.800 --> 00:16:47.980
 They have two separate residencies, a community one and then a master artist program where about 35 people at a time are there, usually visual artists, writers and musicians and the staff.

00:16:48.020 --> 00:16:52.150
 And they all eat together lunch and dinner for three weeks.

00:16:52.290 --> 00:16:57.900
 So their chef for that quit right at the end of my residency and they knew that I had knew how to cook.

00:16:58.060 --> 00:16:59.670
 So and they were desperate.

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:04.400
 So they hired me and flew me in to do the next residency that February.

00:17:04.400 --> 00:17:06.860
 And it went really well.

00:17:06.860 --> 00:17:24.640
 Um, and, uh, now I am the culinary artist in residence, which was my choice to be called that to kind of instead of just a chef for me, it gave me a little leeway and a little bit also, um, flexibility to make the food more artful and to do other side projects.

00:17:24.640 --> 00:17:27.810
 So now I do meal ticket with every group there.

00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:34.090
 And that gets really interesting because there's always a lot of like intimate drama with these groups of people that are living and working together.

00:17:34.560 --> 00:17:38.990
 And there's also always a silo problem between the different groups.

00:17:39.920 --> 00:17:43.660
 Um, so it's a great way to get them together and talk to each other.

00:17:43.660 --> 00:17:52.100
 But, and there's sometimes even awkwardness among the groups or between people for various reasons, because we're humans or because they are culturally really different.

00:17:52.100 --> 00:17:54.260
 So that's been really fun to see.

00:17:54.360 --> 00:18:01.190
 So I just sent out actually, after a couple of years of doing this, I sent out a, just a PDF of the chapters.

00:18:01.510 --> 00:18:07.260
 Each residency is a chapter, um, cause I'm waiting to find funding to actually print it into a book.

00:18:07.560 --> 00:18:17.850
 But some of them, for instance, the year or month that there was comic artists, all of the, uh, recipes are illustrated and they're just amazing.

00:18:17.850 --> 00:18:24.960
 Um, and then the ones with the Pakistani and Indian musicians are really interesting because some of them are like written in Urdu.

00:18:25.280 --> 00:18:33.190
 Um, and, uh, you know, there, there's recipes for love and for music and for, and for different cultural foods.

00:18:33.410 --> 00:18:38.870
 So that's been really another place where I think that this project has really worked.

00:18:39.390 --> 00:18:49.330
 Um, and I would love to actually get them all together and share them with the entire Atlantic Center for the Arts community, both the artists and the community at large.

00:18:49.330 --> 00:18:52.880
 Because I think it's a really good way to get to know who these artists are.

00:18:52.960 --> 00:19:01.590
 If the artists are the heart of this place, then it's a way for the outer community to, I mean, it could even be used as a marketing tool, frankly.

00:19:02.390 --> 00:19:10.470
 Devin interviewed Nikki Ford a while ago, who was, uh, I think her title also was like the culinary artist in residence at Montalvo.

00:19:10.570 --> 00:19:20.080
 She's a chef and I think she, she was an artist, she was a printmaker and then kind of transitioned into being a cook and now is a cook.

00:19:20.080 --> 00:19:24.660
 And she works a lot with artists, but her story is kind of different in terms of what that looks like.

00:19:24.960 --> 00:19:27.820
 And in terms of like thinking about food that really is her craft.

00:19:28.300 --> 00:19:35.510
 But for you, when you talk about it, they sound a lot like you're talking about medium, right?

00:19:35.510 --> 00:19:42.780
 So I know another thing that you like are really interested in, um, is archiving and thinking about how that happened.

00:19:43.000 --> 00:19:47.850
 So something that happens a lot with these food projects is there's no record of them, right?

00:19:47.930 --> 00:19:54.370
 They happen, there's these beautiful meals, there are these performances, they're really intimate, they can be successful and then they're gone.

00:19:54.950 --> 00:19:58.450
 So this, this meal ticket actually like provides some longevity with it.

00:19:58.490 --> 00:19:59.150
 Is that intentional?

00:19:59.390 --> 00:19:59.870
 Absolutely.

00:19:59.870 --> 00:20:11.520
 I mean, in addition to the recipe exchange being a vehicle for conversation, the recipe, the recipe book, it's not only, uh, you know, potentially useful information.

00:20:11.960 --> 00:20:14.640
 It is, it is an archive of our experience.

00:20:14.640 --> 00:20:22.930
 So like, I think that these things are absolutely about archiving and I didn't like realize that link until I started doing so many archive projects.

00:20:22.930 --> 00:20:25.250
 And I was like, oh, actually this totally fits in.

00:20:25.250 --> 00:20:32.230
 And because sometimes I think my work is really coming from disparate places, but, um, in a way like that's always these collections of things.

00:20:32.230 --> 00:20:39.500
 And I'm, I'm like deeply a keeper of stuff, um, as you can tell by looking around the room.

00:20:40.620 --> 00:20:49.860
 So, um, it, uh, yeah, it's really important that, that there's, um, a physical manifestation and memory of it.

00:20:49.860 --> 00:21:07.560
 Well, and one of the things Nikki was also talking about was cooking for people day in and day out and the difference between food that you eat as like a diner when you go to a restaurant versus like what an everyday experience of cooking and eating and living together is.

00:21:07.760 --> 00:21:12.420
 So something about meal ticket kind of is some combination of the two, right?

00:21:12.680 --> 00:21:13.220
 Yeah, sure.

00:21:13.540 --> 00:21:19.370
 Um, it's, it's funny because, um, I don't actually eat when I cook.

00:21:19.810 --> 00:21:27.070
 Um, I do at home, but when I'm cooking for three weeks, you know, 12, 14 hour days, I barely put a morsel in my mouth.

00:21:27.550 --> 00:21:41.620
 And, um, but yet I, I find like my need to kind of nourish and take care of the artists or whoever I'm cooking for, but it's usually artists, um, is so deep.

00:21:41.620 --> 00:21:47.680
 And like the sense of wanting to create a sense of home and a sense of community and a sense of gathering and a sense of communion.

00:21:48.220 --> 00:21:58.100
 Um, it's, um, I don't want to call like everything that I touch art, but there is something that's like deeply embedded in my practice in my job, which is kind of great, you know?

00:21:58.660 --> 00:22:01.340
 Um, and especially just the format of it.

00:22:01.440 --> 00:22:06.220
 It's, um, although I don't do plating cause I'm either alone or have one helper.

00:22:06.750 --> 00:22:11.070
 Uh, it's usually a buffet situation, but, um, it's really casual.

00:22:11.070 --> 00:22:17.170
 So it's not like the panic of a restaurant and it's not like that separation between the kitchen and the dining room.

00:22:17.170 --> 00:22:19.910
 It's like, I'm, I'm out there serving the food and talking to everybody.

00:22:20.350 --> 00:22:23.110
 So, um, there's a, just a real intimacy to it.

00:22:23.390 --> 00:22:37.060
 And then meal ticket is, when I do that is like a sort of like deep culmination of that feeling and, um, and of our whole experience together at the residency, which is also its own med with, with or without me.

00:22:37.100 --> 00:22:38.760
 It's its own magical thing, that residency.

00:22:39.180 --> 00:22:40.600
 Everybody does it, loves it.

00:22:40.600 --> 00:22:45.910
 Well, I've done some residencies where I've been cooked for, and I've done residencies where I haven't been cooked for.

00:22:46.070 --> 00:22:47.340
 And I love to cook.

00:22:47.460 --> 00:22:48.480
 I cook a lot at home.

00:22:48.640 --> 00:22:52.000
 I'm like very invested in what I eat.

00:22:52.100 --> 00:22:57.990
 I care about it, but I love, I love that being cared for part of it.

00:22:58.110 --> 00:23:02.360
 And it's totally different than eating out of the house, right?

00:23:02.440 --> 00:23:12.460
 So if it's done right, it fuels you for whatever creative thing that you're doing and you feel, I don't know, you feel really energized by it.

00:23:12.520 --> 00:23:16.280
 It's like really great to not have to be distracted by what you're going to eat.

00:23:16.800 --> 00:23:28.010
 Um, and I think a lot of times that role just inadvertently like is this unseen role that kind of gets lumped into all the rest of these unseen support roles that go on with residencies.

00:23:28.010 --> 00:23:36.030
 So being a culinary artist and residents coming from the artist world into the, like into the kitchen, that seems like kind of a fun thing to play with.

00:23:36.150 --> 00:23:38.070
 Are there other experiments that you're doing?

00:23:38.630 --> 00:23:40.770
 I kind of, it is totally fun.

00:23:40.890 --> 00:23:49.910
 And, um, I don't really have the energy to do anything but that when I'm there, but it's, it's also totally fulfilling.

00:23:50.510 --> 00:23:56.620
 Um, the, the one thing I do to do, I've, I've done a lot of residencies and I've never been cooked for.

00:23:56.820 --> 00:24:05.010
 And in fact, I usually end up being the one that cooks for everybody, even if it's not my job, which is just my role that I play.

00:24:05.010 --> 00:24:08.900
 And, um, and usually it's a role I'm glad to play.

00:24:08.900 --> 00:24:18.170
 Not always, but, um, I remember cooking for, in Taiwan, I did a four month residency and we all lived together in these two little houses.

00:24:18.170 --> 00:24:24.190
 And there was one other resident that was a really, really good cook and, but otherwise nobody knew how to cook.

00:24:24.310 --> 00:24:27.320
 And so I was just cooking for everybody all the time.

00:24:27.420 --> 00:24:30.940
 Um, but it was our way to gather in the evening and talk about our work and stuff.

00:24:31.020 --> 00:24:38.600
 So, and I got to go to this great market every day that was across the street and, um, try to speak Taiwanese.

00:24:40.740 --> 00:24:49.000
 Um, but at ACA, I don't have time, um, or energy to do anything, but, and then I, the, in my free time, I have to shop.

00:24:49.000 --> 00:24:52.880
 But shopping for that many people is like an epic, epic experience.

00:24:53.060 --> 00:24:58.730
 It's hours and hours of loading and unloading of stuff and organizing stuff and planning.

00:24:59.570 --> 00:25:07.450
 And then on the weekends, when I have them off, I just go out for every meal, which is, I just need to be somebody to take care of me.

00:25:07.810 --> 00:25:14.650
 And I go out for really nice meals and I sit there alone and, um, and it's great.

00:25:17.800 --> 00:25:18.340
 That's awesome.

00:25:18.480 --> 00:25:19.560
 I love that image.

00:25:20.800 --> 00:25:22.160
 Pretty amazing.

00:25:22.650 --> 00:25:28.050
 So we, we made it through a couple of these, the stack of cookbooks.

00:25:28.210 --> 00:25:29.630
 What else have we got down here?

00:25:29.870 --> 00:25:32.130
 We've got a few meal ticket cookbooks.

00:25:32.320 --> 00:25:35.450
 That's the one with, for Christine's project.

00:25:35.870 --> 00:25:36.890
 What was that project?

00:25:37.160 --> 00:25:44.660
 So Christine Wong-yap, who's a great artist who used to be in the Bay Area and now is in New York, um, did a project called Make Things Happen.

00:25:45.100 --> 00:25:51.620
 And she invited a bunch of different artists to give a set of instructions for how to make things or how to make things happen.

00:25:51.820 --> 00:25:55.690
 And those were then designed and printed and given out.

00:25:55.810 --> 00:26:02.190
 And then some of these projects had support roles that could be, um, realized during the, the show.

00:26:02.370 --> 00:26:04.770
 The show was in New York and a couple of different places.

00:26:04.770 --> 00:26:07.070
 And then it was at Interface Gallery in Oakland.

00:26:07.550 --> 00:26:17.160
 So when, for, at Interface, I actually hosted a meal ticket luncheon at the gallery, which is a tiny room, like a third of the size of my living room.

00:26:17.380 --> 00:26:23.250
 So we just had 10 people, I think, and Amanda helped me, Amanda Eicher, which was so great.

00:26:23.830 --> 00:26:28.700
 And, um, we did our, the meal and then the recipe exchange.

00:26:28.700 --> 00:26:40.370
 And we tried to make it both open, but also get a kind of interesting group of people for, when it's that small, you have to, um, you know, it's like curating a dinner party.

00:26:40.510 --> 00:26:41.210
 That's what we did.

00:26:41.250 --> 00:26:46.350
 We curated, we left some of the spaces open, open call, but we also curated some people in.

00:26:46.630 --> 00:26:57.620
 So I curated in a, um, chef slash food, um, teacher, uh, food educator, Nicole Thomas.

00:26:57.620 --> 00:27:07.170
 And then I, uh, curated in a tech worker because I think that that's a really important group of people to be talking to with artists.

00:27:07.630 --> 00:27:22.510
 And, um, and then we had some artists and art writers and, um, I think there were some people of maybe that would identify as other things, but, uh, it was a really interesting group.

00:27:22.510 --> 00:27:28.170
 And, and I just, you know, facilitated the initial conversation and then they were off.

00:27:28.250 --> 00:27:31.540
 And next thing you know, are they still friends?

00:27:32.410 --> 00:27:38.130
 They are some of them, which is, I mean, I sort of, I'm a natural like connector of people and I love doing that.

00:27:38.170 --> 00:27:39.930
 It's one of the most satisfying things ever.

00:27:40.630 --> 00:27:45.290
 Um, I should just start a dating service, frankly, but with really good food.

00:27:46.750 --> 00:28:02.410
 But, uh, but yeah, it was really successful in that way, but it was not like complicated, you know, there, I would say like for that, it was not, um, didn't have the same like larger impact.

00:28:02.410 --> 00:28:09.890
 Act on the world as maybe the meal ticket in Florida or at Portland Art Museum had.

00:28:10.390 --> 00:28:18.100
 However, I'm really a believer that like small, delightful moments are super important and in a way, maybe even political.

00:28:19.700 --> 00:28:21.320
 Yeah. Let's talk about scale for a minute.

00:28:21.640 --> 00:28:24.740
 So that one was really small.

00:28:24.920 --> 00:28:25.740
 That was 10 people.

00:28:26.500 --> 00:28:30.460
 And these other ones seem like they have varied in size.

00:28:30.620 --> 00:28:32.750
 Is that true based on invitation?

00:28:32.750 --> 00:28:37.870
 Yeah. So they, the smallest one was this one for 10 people.

00:28:38.250 --> 00:28:40.650
 The Portland Art Museum was usually 14 or 16.

00:28:41.580 --> 00:28:44.350
 Um, also 14 in Florida.

00:28:44.350 --> 00:28:53.570
 And then the ones, um, the ones I do every month at ACA or every time I do a residency at ACA are usually 35 to 40 people.

00:28:53.950 --> 00:29:03.090
 So it's a big difference and doing, you know, that's about the maximum I want to deal with only because of logistics.

00:29:03.090 --> 00:29:09.430
 Um, if I had lots of help, then let's get thousands, but wouldn't that be cool?

00:29:09.910 --> 00:29:10.630
 That would be amazing.

00:29:11.040 --> 00:29:12.040
 Let me know when you want to do that.

00:29:12.080 --> 00:29:12.440
 I'll help.

00:29:13.100 --> 00:29:22.240
 I have ideas about doing these with like more complicated, awkward, um, groupings of people that might want to punch each other.

00:29:22.340 --> 00:29:26.660
 And so I wonder if those wouldn't be more effective as one-on-one conversations.

00:29:28.100 --> 00:29:36.290
 Um, you know, like what if we're to get, get together, uh, people from polar opposite political views.

00:29:36.410 --> 00:29:46.350
 If you get groups of people from polar opposite political views together, gang mentality can start, you know, whereas if you talk one-on-one, you can get to real stuff.

00:29:46.450 --> 00:29:57.190
 And I learned this from doing a project, um, Ariana Jacob, who's an artist in Portland, did a wonderful project where she interviewed conservative and libertarian people around the country.

00:29:57.190 --> 00:30:00.190
 And I was her documentarian and helped do the interviews.

00:30:00.190 --> 00:30:10.530
 And we realized like when you talk to people one-on-one, you actually get past the, the, uh, political jargon and you actually get to like one-on-one issues.

00:30:10.530 --> 00:30:13.440
 And we all agreed with each other on so many things.

00:30:13.520 --> 00:30:24.970
 Of course, we disagreed on others, but you could actually have a reasonable conversation most of the time about those things and find that, that the, the political divide is like, has, it's like this cage that's come upon us.

00:30:24.970 --> 00:30:33.970
 That is set in place above and over us that doesn't actually exist without, you know, the party system.

00:30:35.910 --> 00:30:36.050
 Yeah.

00:30:36.130 --> 00:30:38.170
 It's, it's, it was a really interesting realization.

00:30:38.170 --> 00:30:41.830
 So there's something to be said for intimate projects.

00:30:43.330 --> 00:30:51.590
 I think there's also something to be said for cooking for people and for acts of generosity in terms of making it so people feel like they can't say no.

00:30:51.910 --> 00:30:52.450
 Right.

00:30:52.450 --> 00:31:00.150
 So, um, it's fun for me to hear you talk about your projects because I read all these other things into these projects.

00:31:00.570 --> 00:31:05.520
 Knowing about them because I think hospitality is so powerful, right?

00:31:05.620 --> 00:31:07.720
 I, I was raised to have good manners.

00:31:07.880 --> 00:31:10.120
 So if you invite me to come eat, I say yes.

00:31:10.810 --> 00:31:17.950
 Like it's even a little hard for me to say no to you offering me tea when there's two choices of tea or water because it's like, is that rude?

00:31:18.930 --> 00:31:19.370
 Right.

00:31:19.490 --> 00:31:29.200
 And so maybe that's just me, but I think lots of people feel sort of, I don't know, like maybe there's this possibility to say yes to something in a way that you wouldn't normally say yes to.

00:31:29.200 --> 00:31:35.630
 And to give that person a chance for an hour and then go back to your other life.

00:31:35.630 --> 00:31:35.990
 Right.

00:31:36.070 --> 00:31:42.290
 So, I know something you've talked about is like this temporary democratic space or a temporary utopic space.

00:31:42.930 --> 00:31:45.360
 And what happens there?

00:31:45.560 --> 00:31:48.340
 I mean, what do you think that disrupts or what do you think that creates?

00:31:48.800 --> 00:31:52.750
 Well, I have a lot of, I'm having a lot of self-doubt lately about whether it does anything.

00:31:53.610 --> 00:31:56.070
 So maybe now's not the best time to talk about that.

00:31:56.070 --> 00:32:21.260
 But I do think that if you create this beautiful situation with beautiful food and potentially beautiful people together that you're sort of disrupting, you're certainly disrupting like a lot of contemporary food culture, which is like I was saying, like shoving something in your mouth while not paying attention to it or not paying attention to the people around you.

00:32:21.640 --> 00:32:26.180
 Even though sharing a meal is this essential thing, I think a lot of times we lose that.

00:32:26.900 --> 00:32:30.730
 And some of it depends on how you grew up.

00:32:30.790 --> 00:32:33.410
 Some of it depends on your life and lifestyle.

00:32:33.790 --> 00:32:47.460
 So, I think getting back to that like essence of sharing a meal at the table and sharing conversation and then, you know, that if it's a beautiful meal that you couldn't have otherwise, that is adding value, you know, temporary value to your life.

00:32:48.510 --> 00:32:50.630
 Is it disrupting anything?

00:32:50.770 --> 00:32:53.410
 Like, is it really just like doing something nice for somebody?

00:32:53.530 --> 00:32:53.950
 I don't know.

00:32:54.290 --> 00:33:01.030
 I think I should never have gone to graduate school because it makes me just be really critical about everything.

00:33:01.740 --> 00:33:11.160
 Well, I think I totally want to talk about that doubt because I think it's, well, I think one of the pitfalls of art school is no tools for measuring, right?

00:33:11.160 --> 00:33:18.850
 So, I think that when you go to grad school for other things, you learn all this methodology where you have tools for measuring impact.

00:33:19.970 --> 00:33:30.890
 And I think as artists, we're supposed to inflate our impact to think that we have like incredible, amazing, profound, God-given impact and no impact, both.

00:33:31.540 --> 00:33:34.180
 But only socially engaged artists are pressured to do that.

00:33:34.540 --> 00:33:37.360
 Like, are you pressured to have impact when you make an abstract painting?

00:33:38.240 --> 00:33:39.400
 Good point.

00:33:41.400 --> 00:33:42.300
 And do you care?

00:33:42.300 --> 00:33:43.360
 Do you care?

00:33:43.480 --> 00:33:47.580
 And are you pressured to measure it yourself?

00:33:48.050 --> 00:33:50.020
 No, you let the critics measure it for you.

00:33:50.960 --> 00:33:57.990
 And that impact is like, doesn't have to do with really real big important stuff.

00:33:58.030 --> 00:34:01.860
 Even though I think art is really big and important no matter what it is.

00:34:02.400 --> 00:34:03.680
 But, you know, there's too much of it.

00:34:03.760 --> 00:34:04.580
 Sure, it's not all good.

00:34:04.680 --> 00:34:10.160
 But like, its existence is super big and important and influences so many things.

00:34:10.640 --> 00:34:23.340
 But yeah, those metrics are really tricky because we are just like constantly, I don't know, like trying to do stuff.

00:34:23.460 --> 00:34:26.160
 And then like, is it changing the world?

00:34:26.650 --> 00:34:28.390
 How do you change the world?

00:34:30.890 --> 00:34:31.330
 Yeah.

00:34:31.470 --> 00:34:32.460
 And I think...

00:34:32.460 --> 00:34:34.040
 One step at a time, I guess.

00:34:35.040 --> 00:34:35.340
 Right.

00:34:35.340 --> 00:34:43.660
 And I think that, is it our job to measure what that impact is?

00:34:43.960 --> 00:35:03.580
 Or is it like, is it like with an abstract painting, like where the job of the artist or this confused job of whatever this artist is, who is you, who is like a cook and like a nurturer and a feminist and maybe somewhere way down the line of those things, an artist.

00:35:03.920 --> 00:35:04.240
 Right.

00:35:04.360 --> 00:35:08.340
 Like, I mean, maybe that's not always the first definition of what you are.

00:35:09.300 --> 00:35:12.190
 Is it your job to also measure that?

00:35:12.330 --> 00:35:14.030
 Is it your job to take that responsibility?

00:35:14.390 --> 00:35:17.490
 Or like the abstract painter, is it your job to make the gesture?

00:35:17.950 --> 00:35:18.790
 Hard to know.

00:35:18.930 --> 00:35:20.030
 Hard to know, for sure.

00:35:20.250 --> 00:35:23.050
 And I would always put artists first in my identity.

00:35:23.690 --> 00:35:30.280
 Although I think with this measuring, sometimes you need to measure as an artist and sometimes you need to measure as an activist.

00:35:30.480 --> 00:35:32.220
 And sometimes you need to measure as a chef.

00:35:32.700 --> 00:35:34.750
 It depends on the scenario.

00:35:35.050 --> 00:35:41.580
 Sometimes you're supposed to measure all of these identities at once and they don't always agree with each other.

00:35:41.580 --> 00:35:48.010
 And does a really good socially engaged artwork then measure high on all of those levels?

00:35:48.460 --> 00:35:48.840
 Can it?

00:35:49.570 --> 00:35:58.230
 You know, can you both like respect the community that you're working with and be aesthetically where you want to be if you've given a voice to those people?

00:35:58.640 --> 00:35:59.530
 Probably not.

00:36:00.420 --> 00:36:04.640
 I can say unilaterally no in my experience of my own projects.

00:36:04.760 --> 00:36:09.870
 I can always identify places where there's room for improvement that way.

00:36:10.430 --> 00:36:12.470
 And it's a hard one, I think.

00:36:12.970 --> 00:36:22.310
 And I think for me, I'm really interested in people who work with food because I think that that is one way to kind of ameliorate some of those risks.

00:36:23.030 --> 00:36:27.250
 That's what I find it as a medium is because people know how to eat.

00:36:27.650 --> 00:36:32.570
 Many people know how to cook, people know how to talk about food, and people have a lot of agency.

00:36:33.290 --> 00:36:38.430
 And it doesn't mean you don't run into all those same other kind of systemic things that we're talking about.

00:36:38.550 --> 00:36:42.710
 But I think, can you think of something else that you could work with?

00:36:42.790 --> 00:36:43.990
 I mean, maybe music is one.

00:36:44.530 --> 00:36:47.090
 I do a bunch of projects with music.

00:36:47.310 --> 00:36:55.320
 Speaking of examples of agency versus aesthetics, I've done a ton of group songwriting projects over the years.

00:36:55.800 --> 00:37:00.100
 And some of them, I collect material from people.

00:37:00.280 --> 00:37:04.620
 And then I will be the aesthetic voice, I and other professional musicians.

00:37:05.000 --> 00:37:10.940
 And we will translate that, their contribution into a professionally made song.

00:37:11.440 --> 00:37:20.560
 Other times, I get a bunch of amateurs together and we make the song and it sounds funny, but it, like, the experience of making it is what's valuable.

00:37:20.840 --> 00:37:23.960
 And I'm always wondering which of these two things has more value.

00:37:24.650 --> 00:37:28.040
 And it's a tricky, I don't think it's answerable.

00:37:28.750 --> 00:37:34.890
 But another thing that I've done is I did a series of jingles with different groups of people.

00:37:34.890 --> 00:37:42.740
 For example, and sometimes asking people, when I was going back to, like, does this have value?

00:37:42.880 --> 00:37:47.960
 Like, asking people to think positively and disrupt cynicism.

00:37:48.380 --> 00:37:54.810
 Like, that is an interesting place where I wonder, like, is disrupting cynicism valuable?

00:37:55.670 --> 00:38:03.010
 And, like, asking people to, like, look deeply and find value in the place that they live and identify that as that.

00:38:03.010 --> 00:38:14.010
 And I always compare it to the Complaints Choir, which is a wonderful Finnish project, which I think is now worldwide, where instead of asking for value, they're asking for complaints.

00:38:14.490 --> 00:38:21.640
 Is that project a lot better than the jingle project because it's voicing negativity as opposed to voicing positivity?

00:38:21.900 --> 00:38:25.120
 Like, do we value negativity and cynicism more than positivity?

00:38:25.680 --> 00:38:28.650
 I think they're both interesting and valuable.

00:38:29.490 --> 00:38:32.810
 Of course, I'd probably put more value on their project because it's not mine.

00:38:32.810 --> 00:38:45.540
 But, I mean, I think we kind of got into the weeds of, like, philosophically, like, how do we value, like, well, how do we value other people's art?

00:38:45.600 --> 00:38:53.120
 And then how do we value and measure and be with our own projects as kind of as they are, right?

00:38:53.220 --> 00:38:59.670
 Like, and when I was looking through your project, there's so many different, you work so many different ways, right?

00:38:59.670 --> 00:39:05.090
 And I feel like there's this spirit kind of experimentation that runs throughout.

00:39:05.410 --> 00:39:19.500
 But especially these ones with food where it's like, what happens when I bring this group of people together and give them a set of instructions and, like, just enough support through this food and these chairs or whatever else?

00:39:20.230 --> 00:39:22.070
 And then I see where it goes.

00:39:22.290 --> 00:39:22.470
 Yeah.

00:39:23.190 --> 00:39:24.940
 I have to tell you, I am an improviser.

00:39:25.170 --> 00:39:26.480
 I'm an improviser as a chef.

00:39:26.480 --> 00:39:27.860
 I'm an improviser as a musician.

00:39:28.580 --> 00:39:32.490
 And I think some artists would cringe if they saw my process.

00:39:33.050 --> 00:39:37.610
 But I have, I think it's so important not to know or plan the outcome.

00:39:38.610 --> 00:39:42.990
 You can have some deliverables that you would like to get out of the experience, like, in your mind.

00:39:43.310 --> 00:39:46.680
 Like, I would like to get some recipes that I could then put in a book.

00:39:47.160 --> 00:39:48.920
 I would like people to talk to each other.

00:39:49.940 --> 00:39:56.860
 Other than that, I'm going to let it fly and let the people who are there make it what it is.

00:39:57.020 --> 00:40:00.990
 I'm not going to control that experience too much.

00:40:01.050 --> 00:40:14.060
 I think it's super important in any socially engaged project that you just make room for those involved to have a voice and to make it what they make it.

00:40:14.160 --> 00:40:15.100
 What happens if you don't?

00:40:15.100 --> 00:40:23.920
 Then you are, I think, using people as materials, which, you know, we say that people are the material of socially engaged art.

00:40:23.980 --> 00:40:34.840
 But I think you're using them and not giving any agency to them, which if you're paying them, maybe that's an okay thing.

00:40:34.900 --> 00:40:39.720
 Like Santiago Sierra, that would, I think that that's really interesting work.

00:40:39.780 --> 00:40:42.050
 And I don't know if they have agency.

00:40:42.050 --> 00:40:44.710
 They're doing what they're asked to do and getting paid for it.

00:40:45.390 --> 00:40:54.500
 So that whole, like, maybe the finances of it change how that level of agency works.

00:40:54.760 --> 00:40:58.520
 But, and with agency also comes responsibility.

00:40:58.780 --> 00:41:05.920
 So are you actually asking people to take responsibility for something that you have the investment in and that you get the cultural capital for?

00:41:06.790 --> 00:41:08.230
 How do you feel about that?

00:41:08.310 --> 00:41:13.410
 That was my next question was, you told me that all your meals are free.

00:41:13.410 --> 00:41:23.700
 And is that, does that kind of uniformly throughout your projects when you ask people to participate, you ask them to participate as you giving this gift to them as a meal?

00:41:24.320 --> 00:41:24.520
 Yeah.

00:41:24.740 --> 00:41:27.600
 And, but these are projects by Lexa Walsh.

00:41:28.040 --> 00:41:28.470
 Yeah.

00:41:28.550 --> 00:41:29.570
 So that's a good point.

00:41:29.650 --> 00:41:36.370
 Like with the music projects, the projects are always by the whatever jingle orchestra and everybody is named.

00:41:37.540 --> 00:41:41.840
 However, I get the cultural capital as the producer of it.

00:41:42.480 --> 00:41:50.540
 And then with the food projects, it's, I think the, that, you know, what they get in exchange is a song that, that they have ownership of, but do they want that?

00:41:50.620 --> 00:41:52.600
 I guess if they didn't want it, they wouldn't participate, right?

00:41:53.450 --> 00:41:55.510
 With the food thing, it's a much clearer exchange.

00:41:55.530 --> 00:42:01.250
 Like you get this meal, you give me this recipe, and then I give you back this cookbook.

00:42:01.430 --> 00:42:05.030
 And, and then I get cultural capital, but, and we are all named in that.

00:42:05.470 --> 00:42:10.790
 I've done other projects where I just ask people if they want to be named or not.

00:42:10.810 --> 00:42:12.590
 And sometimes people don't want to be named.

00:42:12.730 --> 00:42:18.990
 And that's a, and I'm assuming that most people who participate in my projects are happy to do so.

00:42:19.330 --> 00:42:24.450
 However, I'm sure some have not wanted to be and regretted participating.

00:42:25.130 --> 00:42:28.620
 I just can only like imagine that that has been the case.

00:42:28.800 --> 00:42:33.160
 So all I can do is at least respect, like not putting their name if they asked me not to.

00:42:33.340 --> 00:42:34.960
 That has rarely happened, but it has happened.

00:42:35.600 --> 00:42:42.800
 I guess I asked that because I think, well, you're playing with kind of like some interesting cultural tropes, right?

00:42:42.800 --> 00:42:50.460
 This idea that hospitality is freely given, you're kind of problematizing that in this way that I think is kind of interesting.

00:42:51.100 --> 00:42:56.530
 I think this idea that artists are really visible and cooks are really invisible.

00:42:56.950 --> 00:43:00.680
 So you kind of are like, there's some disruption that's happening there.

00:43:01.020 --> 00:43:12.320
 I think this, this other idea of like that, that we don't talk about meals as sites of exchange, even though they really, I mean, what meal is not a site of exchange?

00:43:12.320 --> 00:43:21.040
 Like, even if it's me and my dog, we're still having a lot of exchange, you know, so I should hire you as my manager.

00:43:21.500 --> 00:43:22.070
 Great.

00:43:22.170 --> 00:43:22.690
 I'm available.

00:43:24.410 --> 00:43:28.820
 My job is to read into Lexa's projects for things that she actually doesn't mean.

00:43:29.960 --> 00:43:39.740
 No, but I think one of the problems with social practice is that like we are supposed to be our own interpreters all the time like that.

00:43:39.990 --> 00:43:40.170
 Right.

00:43:40.270 --> 00:43:43.410
 And that these projects, that a gesture is not enough.

00:43:44.480 --> 00:43:44.700
 Right.

00:43:45.040 --> 00:43:52.200
 And it's fun to see other people playing with them in these ways that, I mean, I don't know if I would think all those things are a success or not.

00:43:52.300 --> 00:43:53.420
 It's difficult to say.

00:43:53.520 --> 00:44:03.040
 It's difficult to say what those rubrics are that you're creating, but there's certainly like a lot of experiment and there's a lot of playing with the trouble, which is pretty fun.

00:44:03.910 --> 00:44:09.630
 And yeah, that thing about social practice not being allowed to be a gesture, I think it's really tricky.

00:44:10.190 --> 00:44:16.170
 There's a lot of pressure on us to provide all this content and meaning and activism.

00:44:16.670 --> 00:44:26.340
 And I think about some projects that have been really gesture making, like Paul Ramirez Jonas' key to the city.

00:44:27.140 --> 00:44:46.880
 Um, I actually have the key over there, but you went to Times Square and, um, signed like a sort of passport contract with another person, whoever was in line next to you and you shook hands and were handed this key that opened up, I don't know, 20 different doors or something all around the five boroughs of New York.

00:44:47.120 --> 00:44:54.680
 And it forced you a, to like go to these places that you wouldn't have otherwise gone to in neighborhoods that you don't live or don't visit.

00:44:54.900 --> 00:44:59.860
 And then you had this kind of beautiful gesture at each place.

00:44:59.860 --> 00:45:08.760
 And some of them, I would say that some of them were actually like, you go to open this door, you get a cookie or you go to open this door and there's a little exhibition in a locker in a gym.

00:45:09.020 --> 00:45:18.540
 But the most wonderful one was for me was going to Cabinet Magazine and outside there was a little electrical box that this key opened.

00:45:19.500 --> 00:45:25.180
 And when you opened it up, bubbles and this like sort of tiptoe through the tulipsy kind of song came on.

00:45:26.030 --> 00:45:35.420
 And just that like moment of delight was so, um, just, it was like one of the most profound art experiences I've ever had.

00:45:35.920 --> 00:45:40.740
 And I bring that up all the time to people like, what about, what about delight?

00:45:40.840 --> 00:45:52.130
 And what about gesture that, um, I think that for me that was, it was particularly profound because I had a lot of nostalgia from when I had lived in Brooklyn, um, near that neighborhood.

00:45:52.130 --> 00:46:04.300
 And I hadn't been back for 25 years, but, but it was also actually just, you know, providing me with this little moment of like magic, delight.

00:46:04.300 --> 00:46:13.470
 What about that? Like, why, why don't we value that? Do we value that? Cause I don't feel like we, we value that so much and especially not in social practice.

00:46:13.830 --> 00:46:16.650
 But you value that. So how does that come out in your work?

00:46:17.170 --> 00:46:25.870
 I do value that. And I think it comes out with just maybe even presentation of food or, you know, I think that food brings me that all the time.

00:46:25.870 --> 00:46:37.040
 So good food does. So like trying, trying to give that and, and like actually give love into my food. Sorry to sound so like a hippie, but, um, so yeah, I think it comes across there.

00:46:37.120 --> 00:46:45.720
 Sometimes I'll make some accessories that go along with it that, that, uh, might look like art objects or something to take with you.

00:46:45.900 --> 00:46:56.260
 But I'm constantly struggling with that. Like, is this deep enough or can, you know, where's the, the action versus the gesture or the suggestion of possibility?

00:46:57.160 --> 00:46:59.550
 What if delight was just powerful?

00:47:00.280 --> 00:47:09.530
 What if? I think delight is very powerful personally, but is it, I don't think it's valued among art world thinkers or critics.

00:47:09.530 --> 00:47:16.760
 And do I actually care about them? I'm less and less, but, but I have to care about them because they're the ones who potentially fund my projects.

00:47:17.780 --> 00:47:41.340
 I think that I would like to live in an art world that's more delightful amongst other things, because as I sit here on your couch wearing a black and gray dress, I would actually like a world that's a little bit less that way, you know, that it has a little bit more variability in its, in its capacity for delight, right?

00:47:41.340 --> 00:47:56.780
 It's capacity for nuance that way. And so I think having meals for small amounts of people or big amounts of people where you let them have a little fun and you see the gesture as delightful for you and for them actually does have a lot of room for being a powerful act.

00:47:56.780 --> 00:48:09.240
 I think that the, the art world is a super yucky place. There is a lot of more traditional visual art that does have delight. And that's why I like going to Art Basel Miami. It's all the eye candy is there.

00:48:09.380 --> 00:48:31.570
 Plus you get to look at people with ridiculous shoes and, and, um, cosmetic surgery and just all these, there's just so, so much to experience that that's just really stimulating, even though like the world, the money world behind that and the far cities behind it, it's like, but, and the $300 hotel rooms or more.

00:48:31.970 --> 00:48:53.900
 Yeah, it's got some problems, but there is a lot of, and there's a lot to marvel at, right? And there's like a world full of boondoggles that should be enjoyed at least for that one week. Well, that they all got shipped there from all over the world for some reason. It's pretty amazing. Um, where can we follow along with what you do and what's up next for you?

00:48:53.980 --> 00:49:16.840
 Oh my goodness. Well, I should say that I'm going through this funny period right now where I am trying to make objects again. So when I first went to grad school, I had this amazing studio visit with Mel Ziegler, the public artist, and he was looking at all my past work. And I'd been practicing art for a long time. And I, you know, I didn't go to grad school till I was 41. Um, so he's looking at all my work and he's like, you know,

00:49:16.980 --> 00:49:43.910
 it seems like all these objects are kind of accessorizing the essence of the work, which is about generosity and reciprocity and various other things. And just letting you know that that's what I think. And so I thought, oh, maybe I'll go for a year without making an object, which then turned into five years. So I really focused on like getting to the essence of the practice and the exchange and the experience and didn't make anything or performative work, but didn't make any like physical stuff to go with it.

00:49:44.330 --> 00:50:12.560
 And then I realized that I was turning into an event planner, that my practice was basically writing and reading emails and having meetings. And it was incredibly boring. Even if like the work may or may not be interesting or good to other people, my experience making it sucked. So I then, uh, had this amazing opportunity to do a project with my brother this year at the Williams College Museum of Art. And he is a painter. And so we collaborated to make

00:50:12.560 --> 00:50:36.290
 these giant object kiosks housing for the collection of Williams. And so we, it ended up being like this major, massive project. And then we also made, uh, stitch samplers that had various methods to relay our different art theories on them. To make a long story short, it was an incredible experience to make stuff again. I'm just like, yes.

00:50:36.290 --> 00:50:58.980
 Yes. So, uh, what's been up is me working in the studio, trying to figure out how, how to make stuff, but how to make stuff that still has meaning and that how maybe I will incorporate my practice and then turn it back into an object or somehow use an object within it. So that's experimentation continues.

00:50:58.980 --> 00:51:05.560
 Cool. That sounds, that sounds exciting. I'll look forward to seeing what happens next.

